  "title": {
    "en": "Terns"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Sandwich, Common, Arctic, Roseate, Little, Black, White-winged and Caspian Tern"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Terns are the elegant relatives of the gulls. They are lighter, they fly with their bill pointing down, and they dive for fish. In summer you find them on the beach and the pits, and in autumn the juveniles gather in estuaries before the long flight to West Africa and beyond. Arctic Terns winter in Antarctica.\n\nThe big trio of Common, Arctic and Roseate Tern is the one that decides a birder's skill: they are almost the same size, nearly the same colour, and they differ in small things, like the bill, the tail and the call. Then come the others: Little Tern, the small one, Sandwich Tern, the big one with a shaggy crest, and the marsh terns, Black and White-winged.\n\nMay to August is the time. In spring the Common and Arctic Terns arrive in late April and May, with the biggest numbers going through on the coast. Autumn passage runs from August to October. Black Terns pass inland in May and again in August and September."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "tail streamers"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The long outer tail feathers of an adult tern. They reach past the wing tip in Arctic and Roseate Terns."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "cap"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The black crown of a tern. In autumn it shrinks to a black mask with a white forehead."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "translucent"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "Light shines through. The flight feathers of an Arctic Tern look glassy when seen against the sky."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "marsh tern"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A tern of fresh water: Black, White-winged and Whiskered. They pick insects from the surface instead of diving."
      }
    }
  ],
